    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Proclaimed emperor by his father's army at York in 306, Constantine won the civil wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> against Maxentius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> and ruled the whole empire from 324.</li><li>He ended the persecution of Christians, legalised their religion with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> in 313, convoked the Council of Nicaea and built churches such as the Holy Sepulchre, though scholars still debate the depth of his own faith.</li><li>His new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and his gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a> outlasted him by centuries, while his dynastic succession replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> and his reputation has swung between saint and ruthless politician.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a> Constantine introduced, 72 of which made a pound of gold, was struck at a similar weight and purity for seven centuries.</p></aside>
    </section>

