    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">272</span><span class="rf-text">Born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nis">Naissus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> Superior, the son of Flavius Constantius and Helena</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">306</span><span class="rf-text">On his father's death at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>), proclaimed augustus by the army</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">312</span><span class="rf-text">Defeats Maxentius at the Battle of the Milvian Bridge and enters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">313</span><span class="rf-text">Agrees with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on the edict granting tolerance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and all religions</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">324</span><span class="rf-text">Defeats <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> at Chrysopolis, becomes sole emperor and founds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">325</span><span class="rf-text">Convokes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a>, which produces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">326</span><span class="rf-text">Has his eldest son Crispus put to death and his wife Fausta killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">337</span><span class="rf-text">Baptised on his deathbed by Eusebius of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, dies near Nicomedia and is buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Constantine was born on 27 February 272 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nis">Naissus</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> Superior, now Niš in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>; his birthday is recorded, but not the year, which scholars have placed between 271 and 280. His father, Flavius Constantius, was an officer in Emperor Aurelian's bodyguard who rose to be governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>. His mother, Helena, was a Greek woman of low social standing, possibly from Drepanum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, and it is uncertain whether she was Constantius's wife or his concubine. Around 288 or 289 Constantius left her to marry Theodora, the stepdaughter of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a>, and in 293 he was made a caesar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a>.</p><p>Constantine was sent to Diocletian's court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> as his father's heir presumptive and, since the tetrarchs did not trust one another, as something of a hostage. There he learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> literature, Greek and philosophy, and may have heard the Christian scholar Lactantius; his own language was Latin, and he later needed translators at church councils held in Greek. He campaigned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in 296, against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in 297 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> in 298–299. He was in Nicomedia in 303 when Diocletian began the Great Persecution, the most severe persecution of Christians in Roman history. He probably played no part in it, and nothing shows that he opposed it effectively at the time.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximian">Maximian</a> abdicated on 1 May 305, Constantine and Maximian's son Maxentius were passed over. Ancient sources tell of plots by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galerius">Galerius</a> against his life, though how far these tales can be trusted is uncertain. Constantine left the court to join his father, campaigned with him against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, and when Constantius died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eboracum">Eboracum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>) on 25 July 306 the army proclaimed him augustus. Galerius, furious, granted him only the rank of caesar. Ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, he threw back a Frankish invasion and had the captured kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascaric">Ascaric</a> and Merogais and their soldiers fed to the beasts in the city's amphitheatre.</p><p>In 307 he married Maximian's daughter Fausta. When Maximian rebelled against him in 310, Constantine captured him at Massilia and encouraged his suicide; Maximian hanged himself in July 310, and his memory was condemned. A claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius-gothicus">Claudius II</a> and a vision of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, proclaimed in a speech of 310 and very probably fictions, gave Constantine a new title to rule. In the spring of 312 he crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> with about 40,000 men, won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> and Verona, and on 28 October met Maxentius at the Milvian Bridge. Lactantius says a dream told him to mark his soldiers' shields with the sign of Christ; Eusebius describes a cross of light in the sky. Maxentius drowned in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiber-river">Tiber</a>, and his head was paraded through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> Constantine disbanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praetorian-guard">Praetorian Guard</a> and promised the Senate a secure role. In 313 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> agreed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on the edict granting full tolerance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and all religions and returning property seized in the persecution. The two emperors fell out and fought at Cibalae in 314 or 316 and at Mardia in 317; in 324 Constantine won at Adrianople, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a> and Chrysopolis and became sole emperor. That year he founded his new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, dedicated on 11 May 330 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><p>He gave the Church money and privileges, promoted Christians to high office and built basilicas, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-holy-sepulchre">Church of the Holy Sepulchre</a> and Old St Peter's. In 321 he made Sunday a day of rest, and in 325 he summoned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a>, which dealt with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arianism">Arianism</a> and produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-creed">Nicene Creed</a>. His reign set a precedent for emperors' authority over church councils. He also reformed the state: civil and military authorities were separated, the army was divided into mobile comitatenses and frontier limitanei, many offices were raised to senatorial rank in 326, and the gold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a>, 72 to the pound, became the standard coin. Between 331 and 336 the gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and bronze statues of pagan temples were declared imperial property and melted down for coinage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Constantine could be ruthless with his rivals and his own family. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licinius">Licinius</a> and Martinian surrendered on his promise to spare them, but in 325 he accused Licinius of plotting and had both hanged; Licinius's son was killed in 326. In 326 his eldest son Crispus was put to death by "cold poison" at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pula">Pola</a>, and in July his wife Fausta was killed in an overheated bath. Few ancient sources discuss why, and those that do are late and generally unreliable; Adrian Goldsworthy speculates that Constantine wished to keep a firm grip on his heirs. He also used force against Christians he judged schismatic, ordering in 317 that the property of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatism">Donatists</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> be confiscated and their clergy exiled, and some of his laws on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were unfavourable, though not harsher than his predecessors'. His panegyrist declared that "It is a stupid clemency that spares the conquered foe."</p><p>His critics began in his own family. His nephew Julian the Apostate called him inferior to the great pagan emperors and given over to luxury and greed, and Eunapius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zosimus">Zosimus</a> blamed him for weakening the empire through indulgence to the Christians. The author of De rebus bellicis held that his monetary policy widened the gap between rich and poor. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-gibbon">Edward Gibbon</a> described him as a war hero "degenerating into a cruel and dissolute monarch" in old age, and Jacob Burckhardt saw a scheming secularist who manipulated every party to secure his power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Constantine lived much of his later life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In 328 a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at Sucidava was completed in the hope of reconquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dacia">Dacia</a>; he campaigned with the Sarmatians against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a> in 332 and against the Sarmatians themselves in 334, settling some of their exiles as farmers and conscripting the rest, and he took the title Dacicus maximus in 336. When Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narseh">Narseh</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in 336 and set a Persian client on its throne, Constantine prepared a war he treated as a Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> to accompany the army and a tent in the shape of a church. He turned away Persian envoys seeking peace, but the campaign was called off when he fell ill in the spring of 337.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> he went to the baths near Helenopolis, then to the suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a>, where he asked for baptism, which he had long put off; the Arian bishop Eusebius of Nicomedia baptised him. He died at the villa of Achyron on 22 May 337. Eusebius places his death after the Persian campaign, while most other sources put it in the middle of the preparations, and some scholars conclude that the Vita was edited to defend his reputation. He was buried in a porphyry sarcophagus in the Church of the Holy Apostles. His three sons by Fausta, Constantine II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantius-ii">Constantius II</a> and Constans, succeeded him, and followers of Constantius killed several relatives, among them the nephews Dalmatius and Hannibalianus.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Constantine replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> with dynastic succession, and the age of Constantine marked a turn from classical antiquity towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> remained the capital of the empire for more than a thousand years, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidus</a> was struck at a similar weight and purity for seven centuries. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> it became a great honour to be hailed as a "new Constantine", and ten emperors bore his name; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> and several dynasties claimed descent from him. The Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> and Eastern Catholic Churches venerate him as isapostolos, equal of the Apostles, with his mother Helena on 21 May. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donation-of-constantine">Donation of Constantine</a>, an 8th-century document in which he grants <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the West to Pope Sylvester, was long accepted as the basis of papal temporal power until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-valla">Lorenzo Valla</a> proved it a forgery in 1440.</p><p>The sources are abundant but shaped by official propaganda. Eusebius's Vita Constantini, written between 335 and 339, mixes eulogy and hagiography, and modern historians have often challenged its reliability. The medieval church held Constantine up as a paragon, while the rediscovery of hostile sources in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> brought more critical views. Modern scholars still disagree about his faith: Henri Grégoire thought he took up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> for its political use, Timothy Barnes saw a radical convert on a personal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a>, and Peter Heather argues that he was raised a Christian and revealed it in stages.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Constantine rose from his father's army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to win the civil wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetrarchy">Tetrarchy</a> and rule the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> from 324. He legalised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, convoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">Council of Nicaea</a> and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and he also killed his rivals, his son and his wife; ancient propaganda and modern scholarship have left his faith and his motives disputed.</em></p>

